{
  "image_name": "58d1671d10610f865e27cb0e263decbd.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes printed organic device fabrication and the barrier performance needed for flexible packaging and electronics. One schematic shows the layered structure of a patterned thin-film device on glass: a transparent ITO electrode, a light-absorbing layer, and a back contact, with patterned regions and widths marked to define active and dead areas. The rest of the figure illustrates common roll-to-roll or solution-coating methods used to deposit such layers on moving substrates.\n\nThree coating/printing approaches are depicted. In one, ink is dispensed ahead of a blade and spread into a thin film as the substrate moves. In another, a coating head forms a controlled meniscus over a rotating support, again creating a uniform wet layer. A gravure setup uses an engraved cylinder that picks up ink from a bath, is metered by a doctor blade, and transfers patterned ink to a substrate through an impression cylinder.\n\nThe final plot compares oxygen and water vapor transmission rates. It shows that ordinary polymer films such as PET/PEN have relatively poor barrier properties, food-packaging barriers are better, and organic electronics require much lower permeation still. This highlights the need for ultrahigh barrier materials for sensitive printed electronic devices.",
